# [DD-13b.W23.3, D100] §2.22's NARROWING, PINNED — and it is a narrowing
# CHOSEN rather than forced (§1.6.1a case (5)): this file COMPILES on the
# pre-W23.3 binary, with a byte-identical artifact, because `(?&x_y)`
# bound to the definition literally spelled `x_y` and the hyphenated one
# beside it was simply unreachable.
#
# Under the derived-identifier binding BOTH definitions derive `x_y`, so
# the call is a refusal naming BOTH definitions and the shared
# identifier. EXACT SPELLING DOES NOT WIN: "exact" is only the identity
# case of the same mapping, and a silent tie-break would make the
# mapping's non-injectivity free exactly where it bites.
#
# A refusal naming only the prefix is a RED — the whole content of the
# rule is that an author is told which two names collided, because the
# repair is renaming one of them and the prefix does not say which.
#
# LEG A ONLY: legs B and C resolve no calls at all, so there is nothing
# for them to agree or disagree about. The refusal arrives through
# `--source` (the composer), not through `--list-source` (which reports
# the file as written and binds nothing).
target rx = caller

pattern [0-9]
name x-y

pattern [a-z]
name x_y

pattern ^(?&x_y)$
name caller
features recursion,named-groups
